D = "ivx_interpersonal"
ITEMS = [
dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan security operations", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="As you approach the topic of his mother, a patient suddenly becomes jocular, asks about your accent, and says the room is too warm. In Sullivan’s terms, what are you most likely observing?",
a="Security operations: maneuvers that reduce rising anxiety by deflecting the conversation from a threatening area",
ae="Sullivan called the self-system’s anxiety-reducing maneuvers security operations. They include changes of subject, humor, irrelevant complaints and sudden interest in the interviewer. For Sullivan, these maneuvers mark exactly where the important material lies, and the interviewer’s job is to notice and track them, not to bulldoze through.",
w=[("Thought disorder emerging under emotional load, specifically tangentiality",
"Tangentiality is a formal thought disorder, typically persistent and not tied to a particular topic. Here the shift is topic-specific and goal-directed toward reducing anxiety."),
("A dissociative episode triggered by traumatic recall",
"Dissociation involves altered awareness, detachment or memory gaps. Joking and asking about your accent are interpersonal maneuvers, not altered consciousness."),
("Hypomanic distractibility revealing an underlying bipolar disorder",
"Hypomanic distractibility is diffuse and accompanied by other signs (pressured speech, elevated energy). This shift appears only near a specific subject.")],
x="Sullivan saw anxiety as the central force shaping interpersonal life and the interview itself. The interviewer’s task is to keep anxiety at a level that allows exploration: too little and nothing new emerges; too much and security operations take over. Sullivan described the interviewer as a participant observer who also watches his own contribution to the patient’s anxiety.",
s="Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview; The Interpersonal Theory of Psychiatry",
h="Security operations = the patient’s “anxiety alarm system.” Where the alarm rings, the treasure lies."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan selective inattention", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Sullivan described a process by which people fail to notice aspects of their experience that would provoke anxiety, although these aspects are not deeply repressed. What did he call it, and what does it mean for the interviewer?",
a="Selective inattention; the interviewer must also watch for what he himself is not noticing because it makes him anxious",
ae="Selective inattention is Sullivan’s term for the routine, near-conscious failure to register anxiety-laden experience. It applies to the interviewer as well: clinicians skip over topics that unsettle them (sexuality, money, violence, death). Sullivan’s participant observation demands awareness of the interviewer’s own blind spots.",
w=[("Dissociation proper; the interviewer must use grounding techniques",
"Sullivan did use the term dissociation, but for more profound exclusion of experience from awareness. Selective inattention is the lighter, everyday version."),
("Repression; the interviewer must interpret the defense early to lift it",
"Repression is Freud’s concept. Sullivan’s selective inattention is not about deep repression, and early interpretation is not what he recommended."),
("Negative hallucination; the interviewer should test for psychosis",
"Negative hallucination (not perceiving what is present) is a different phenomenon, linked to hypnosis and severe psychopathology.")],
x="A practical exercise: after an interview, list topics you did not ask about. Patterns emerge (a trainee who never asks about sex, another who never asks about money, another who avoids the patient’s anger at them). That pattern is your selective inattention. Sullivan’s statement that we are all much more simply human than otherwise was meant to reduce the interviewer’s anxiety and thus his selective inattention.",
s="Sullivan, The Interpersonal Theory of Psychiatry (1953)",
h="Selective inattention = “the spotlight skips what scares.” Check where your own spotlight skips."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx one genus postulate", m="say", l=2,
q="Sullivan’s “one-genus postulate” is often quoted. Which interviewing stance follows directly from it?",
a="Approaching even the most disturbed patient as fundamentally human, so their experiences are understandable in principle",
ae="Sullivan said that we are all much more simply human than otherwise. In interviewing, this means approaching psychosis or perversion not as alien but as variations of human processes. This lowers the interviewer’s anxiety and contempt, which in turn lowers the patient’s.",
w=[("Using a single standardized structured interview for all patients so comparisons are valid",
"This confuses “one genus” with standardization. Sullivan was anti-mechanical and emphasized the particular patient."),
("Diagnosing within one diagnostic category before considering comorbidity",
"The postulate has nothing to do with diagnostic hierarchy."),
("Treating schizophrenia as a purely biological disease separate from normal experience",
"Sullivan held the opposite view, seeing schizophrenia as interpersonally meaningful.")],
x="Sullivan worked extensively with young men with schizophrenia at Sheppard Pratt, using specially trained staff, and reported high recovery rates for the time. His humane stance shaped Frieda Fromm-Reichmann, who carried it to Chestnut Lodge, and later Harold Searles and Leston Havens.",
s="Sullivan, The Interpersonal Theory of Psychiatry",
h="One genus: “We are all one species of sorrow.” If you can’t imagine yourself there, you haven’t looked hard enough."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx fromm reichmann experience not explanation", m="say", l=2,
q="A patient with long-standing schizoid withdrawal asks why he feels nothing for anyone. Following the spirit of Frieda Fromm-Reichmann, which is the best stance?",
a="Prioritize a lived, reliable experience of relating in the session over intellectual explanations of his detachment",
ae="Fromm-Reichmann’s often-quoted dictum is that the patient needs an experience, not an explanation. Schizoid and psychotic patients can absorb interpretations intellectually without change. A consistent, respectful, non-intrusive relationship is the therapeutic agent.",
w=[("Explain the developmental origins of his detachment early so he gains insight",
"Early explanation tends to be absorbed intellectually. It may confirm his sense that relating is a mental exercise."),
("Push him to express feelings so the affect can be discharged",
"Pressuring a schizoid patient to feel often increases withdrawal and leads to a sense of intrusion."),
("Refer him to group therapy at once, as individual work will reinforce isolation",
"Group may help later, but immediate referral bypasses the relational experience that Fromm-Reichmann prioritized.")],
x="Fromm-Reichmann, in Principles of Intensive Psychotherapy (1950), emphasized listening, respect for the patient’s pace, and the therapist’s awareness of his own anxieties and needs. Her treatment of Joanne Greenberg became the basis of the novel I Never Promised You a Rose Garden, where she appears as Dr. Fried.",
s="Fromm-Reichmann, Principles of Intensive Psychotherapy (1950)",
h="Fromm-Reichmann: “FROM explanation TO experience.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx fromm reichmann listening", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="Fromm-Reichmann warned about a characteristic error of the eager interviewer. Which one?",
a="Understanding too quickly, filling the patient’s statements with the interviewer’s own meanings",
ae="She urged therapists to tolerate not knowing and to listen without premature conclusions. Quick understanding often means the interviewer has projected his own frame, and the patient learns that his particular experience is not wanted.",
w=[("Being too silent, which she considered always harmful to schizophrenic patients",
"She advised careful use of silence, not its total avoidance. With some patients, quiet presence was helpful."),
("Showing too little interest in the patient’s dreams",
"Dreams were not her central concern regarding interviewing errors."),
("Taking too long to establish a diagnosis",
"She was relatively unconcerned with diagnostic speed compared with understanding.")],
x="This links to Bion’s later instruction to approach each session without memory or desire, and to the mentalization-based stance of not knowing. Across schools, the expert interviewer resists the urge to file the patient into a familiar category too soon.",
s="Fromm-Reichmann, Principles of Intensive Psychotherapy",
h="Fast understanding is often your story told in their words. Slow down until it’s their story."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx havens counterprojective statement", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="A paranoid patient is convinced his boss is plotting against him and keeps glaring at you as if you might be part of it. Leston Havens described a type of statement for this situation. Which one is a counter-projective statement?",
a="“A boss like that, watching every move you make, would put anyone on edge.”",
ae="Havens’ counter-projective statements direct attention, the patient’s and the clinician’s, toward the figure “out there,” alongside the patient. They move the projected figure away from the interviewer. The clinician stands beside the patient looking at the threatening object, instead of being in front of him as a possible persecutor.",
w=[("“I wonder if you’re worried that I might be on your boss’s side.”",
"This is a transference interpretation. It brings the projection into the room and focuses it on the clinician, which can intensify paranoia early on."),
("“There’s no evidence your boss is plotting against you.”",
"Direct contradiction of a delusional or overvalued idea usually strengthens it and the clinician becomes an adversary."),
("“How long have you felt people at work are against you?”",
"This is a reasonable history question, but it is not a counter-projective statement and does not deal with the projection onto you.")],
x="Havens, in Making Contact (1986), described a language of interviewing: empathic statements (simple and complex), interpersonal statements (including counter-projective and anti-introjective statements), and performative statements. Counter-projective technique, rooted in Sullivan’s work with paranoid patients, places the clinician beside the patient, both facing outward toward the persecutory object.",
s="Havens, Making Contact: Uses of Language in Psychotherapy (1986)",
h="Counter-projective = “side by side, both looking out the window.” Don’t sit where the persecutor sits."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx havens performative", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="Havens described “performative” statements. Which of these is one?",
a="“You are welcome here, exactly as you are today.”",
ae="Performative statements, in J. L. Austin’s sense, do something rather than describe something: they welcome, apologize, promise or affirm a person’s place. For patients who have been rejected or diminished, a performative creates a small piece of new reality in the relationship.",
w=[("“You seem to be feeling unwelcome.”",
"This is an empathic statement, reflecting the patient’s feeling. It describes, rather than performs."),
("“Many people in your situation feel unwelcome in clinics.”",
"This is normalization. It reduces shame but does not perform an act of welcome."),
("“I wonder if you expect me to reject you like your father did.”",
"This is an interpretation linking past and present. It is not performative.")],
x="Havens drew on speech act theory (Austin, How to Do Things with Words). Performative statements are powerful but can feel hollow if overused or insincere. They are especially useful with patients who have been degraded by harsh introjects or traumatic experiences, where explanation is insufficient and a lived act of respect is needed.",
s="Havens, Making Contact (1986); Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962)",
h="Performative = words that DO something: “I welcome,” “I apologize,” “I promise.” A spoken act, not a spoken fact."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx havens anti introjective", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="A depressed woman says, “I’m lazy and useless; I can’t even keep my kitchen clean.” Havens would describe which response as anti-introjective?",
a="“Who taught you that a messy kitchen makes a person useless?”",
ae="Anti-introjective statements challenge the harsh internalized voice (the introject), separating it from the patient’s self and questioning its authority. Asking who taught her that rule locates the harsh judgment as something learned and external in origin, rather than a fact about her.",
w=[("“You must feel really bad about yourself right now.”",
"This is a simple empathic statement. It validates feeling but leaves the introject untouched."),
("“Let’s look at the evidence for and against the idea that you’re lazy.”",
"This is a cognitive restructuring move. It argues with the content of the belief rather than the internal voice issuing it."),
("“A lot of people with depression find housework very hard.”",
"This is psychoeducational normalization, which reduces shame but does not target the introject.")],
x="Havens argued that some patients are dominated by internal figures (harsh parents, abusers, religious authorities) whose voices they mistake for their own judgment. Anti-introjective technique gives the patient an ally against that voice. It overlaps with schema therapy’s confrontation of the punitive parent mode and with compassion-focused therapy’s work on the inner critic.",
s="Havens, Making Contact (1986)",
h="Anti-introjective: “Whose voice is that?” Put the critic on trial, not the patient."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx havens four approaches", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="In Approaches to the Mind (1973), Havens described four major schools of psychiatry, each implying a different interviewing stance. Which stance is matched correctly?",
a="Existential approach: the interviewer tries to be with the patient, sharing the experience rather than standing outside it",
ae="Havens contrasted the objective-descriptive (observer outside the patient, cataloguing signs), psychoanalytic (listening to free association for hidden meaning), interpersonal (participant observation, Sullivan) and existential (being with the patient, Binswanger, Laing). Each yields different data. The existential stance seeks shared presence over detached observation.",
w=[("Objective-descriptive approach: the interviewer interprets resistance in the here and now",
"The descriptive school, from Kraepelin onward, observes and classifies signs and symptoms. Interpreting resistance belongs to the psychoanalytic approach."),
("Psychoanalytic approach: the interviewer stands beside the patient, facing outward toward persecutors",
"Standing beside the patient facing outward is Havens’ description of interpersonal and counter-projective work, not classical psychoanalytic technique."),
("Interpersonal approach: the interviewer catalogues symptoms without personal involvement",
"Sullivan’s interpersonal approach insists on participant observation: the interviewer is involved and his participation is part of the data.")],
x="Havens argued that expert clinicians can move between these stances during one interview, choosing the one the moment calls for. Many of Havens’ later techniques (counter-projective, anti-introjective, performative) are attempts to operationalize the interpersonal and existential stances in specific language.",
s="Havens, Approaches to the Mind (1973)",
h="Havens’ four seats: Observer (outside), Analyst (behind), Participant (beside), Existentialist (with)."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx searles therapeutic symbiosis", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Harold Searles, working at Chestnut Lodge, described a phase in the treatment of chronic schizophrenia in which the boundaries between therapist and patient feel blurred and the therapist experiences intense dependence and fusion. How did he regard this phase?",
a="As a necessary therapeutic symbiosis, from which individuation could later emerge",
ae="Searles saw therapeutic symbiosis as an essential phase in work with severely ill patients, mirroring early mother-infant symbiosis. The therapist’s experience of fusion, confusion and intense feeling was understood as data and as part of the process, not as a failure of boundaries in itself.",
w=[("As an iatrogenic error that should be stopped at once by referral",
"Searles did not see symbiosis as an error to stop, although he insisted on self-examination and supervision."),
("As a sign that the patient had developed a manic switch",
"Searles described this as a relational phase, not a mood state."),
("As evidence that antipsychotic medication was needed to restore boundaries",
"Searles worked largely before or alongside the medication era and did not frame this phase in pharmacological terms.")],
x="Searles’ papers, collected in Collected Papers on Schizophrenia and Related Subjects (1965), include “The effort to drive the other person crazy” and “Oedipal love in the countertransference.” He wrote unusually candidly about his own feelings, including hatred, love and boredom, as crucial instruments. Modern interviewers can borrow his insistence that the clinician’s experience is part of the clinical data.",
s="Searles, Collected Papers on Schizophrenia and Related Subjects (1965)",
h="Searles: “Seer of the SEAms” — he watched the seams between therapist and patient open and close."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan anxiety gradient", m="ask", l=2,
q="Sullivan held that the interviewer should manage the patient’s anxiety during the interview. What is the target level?",
a="Moderate anxiety: enough that the patient engages meaningfully, not so much that security operations block communication",
ae="Too little anxiety and the interview remains superficial; too much and the patient deflects, freezes or leaves. The interviewer adjusts pace, transitions and tone to stay in the productive range.",
w=[("Zero anxiety, so that the patient feels completely relaxed throughout",
"An anxiety-free interview often avoids everything important. Some anxiety signals that meaningful territory has been reached."),
("High anxiety, because defenses break down and truth emerges under pressure",
"This is close to an interrogation model, and Sullivan explicitly rejected it. High anxiety produces security operations, not truth."),
("Anxiety level is irrelevant if the interviewer asks the right questions",
"For Sullivan, anxiety was the central variable in every interpersonal process, including the interview.")],
x="This idea resembles the Yerkes–Dodson curve applied to disclosure, though Sullivan did not frame it that way. In practice, the expert interviewer reads micro-signals (pauses, shifts, humor, a hand to the face) and adjusts. Shea’s validity techniques can be seen as tools for keeping anxiety in the workable range while approaching shame-laden material.",
s="Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview",
h="Anxiety thermostat: “warm enough to move, not so hot they flee.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan participant observer", m="eye", l=2,
q="What did Sullivan mean by calling the interviewer a “participant observer”?",
a="The interviewer inevitably affects what he observes, so his own participation must be part of what he studies",
ae="Sullivan rejected the idea of a neutral observer. The interviewer’s manner, anxieties and reactions shape what the patient says. Good interviewing means watching both the patient and the interaction, including oneself.",
w=[("The interviewer participates in the patient’s daily life outside sessions to observe it directly",
"Participant observation in anthropology involves living with the group, but Sullivan used the phrase for the interview relationship."),
("The interviewer should participate only after observing silently for the first half",
"Sullivan did not prescribe silence followed by participation."),
("Interviewers should be observed by colleagues while participating, as a training requirement",
"Supervision is valuable, but this is not what the term means.")],
x="Participant observation anticipates later relational psychoanalysis and two-person psychology (Mitchell, Aron), as well as the modern understanding of enactments. It also has a practical edge: when an interview goes badly, ask what you did, not only what the patient is like.",
s="Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview",
h="Participant observer: “You’re in the photograph you’re taking.”"),
]
